    "body":  "## Purpose\n\nA full HF3FS page pool makes every save fail permanently: once\n`allocate_pages_for_keys` runs out of pages there is no way to get one back and\nthe metadata server has to be restarted (issue #62389).\n\n#62393 stops cache hits and duplicate keys from consuming *new* pages, but it\ndeliberately does not add eviction, so the failure above remains.\n\nNaively evicting the least-recently-used key is unsafe here: the scheduler calls\n`batch_key_exists` (via `get_num_new_matched_tokens`) before the worker loads the\nblock (`get_key_locations`), so a key can be a \"hit\" while its page is still\nabout to be read. Recycling that page turns a hit into a load failure or into\nanother request\u0027s data.\n\n## Stacked on #62393\n\nThis branch is based on #62393 (`84f00fa`) because the two changes touch the same\nallocation path. The first commit in the diff is #62393 itself; **only the second\ncommit is new here**. I will rebase onto `main` once #62393 lands.\n\n## What this does\n\n- **Reader leases.** `POST /keys/acquire` returns page locations *and* pins the\n  keys; `POST /keys/release` drops the pin. The load path\n  (`AsyncOperationManager._handle_load_task`) acquires before reading and\n  releases as soon as the blocks are in host buffers: on success, on a missing\n  key, on read failure, and on exceptions.\n- **LRU eviction of safe keys only.** When allocation needs more pages than the\n  free list has, the server reclaims complete, unleased keys that are not part of\n  the allocating request (`exclude=cached_pages`), oldest first, returning one\n  page per rank to the free list and deleting the key.\n- **Lease expiry.** Leases carry a monotonic deadline (default 120 s,\n  `--lease-ttl-s`) so a crashed reader cannot pin pages forever. The TTL is a\n  safety parameter, not a tuning knob: it has to exceed the slowest block load.\n  Reported figures in #62389: a 28k-token prompt loads in about 1.1 s and a 60k\n  one in about 1.9 s, but a failed 3FS storage pod stalled loads for 16-18 s, so\n  the 120 s default leaves roughly 7x headroom over the observed stall. Renewal\n  for loads longer than the TTL is not implemented; a reader that stalls longer\n  than the TTL is treated as dead.\n- **Backward compatible.** `Hf3fsMetadataInterface.acquire_keys/release_keys`\n  have non-abstract defaults (plain lookup / no-op), so the mock and single-node\n  clients are unaffected. The HTTP client falls back to `get_key_locations` on\n  404/405, which is safe against an older server because it never evicts.\n\n## Correctness argument\n\n- A page is only handed out after it is back in the free list; eviction releases\n  it there first.\n- Only complete keys are tracked for eviction; partial keys stay owned by their\n  writer (the boundary with #61247).\n- A key with a live lease is never evicted; its owner releases it or it expires.\n- Eviction cannot target the allocating request\u0027s own hits, so a batch that has\n  already decided to read a page cannot recycle it underneath itself.\n\n## Testing\n\n- `tests/v1/kv_connector/unit/test_hf3fs_metadata_server.py` gains 7 cases:\n  eviction makes room, leases protect, expired leases are reclaimed, partial keys\n  are skipped, same-request hits are not evicted, LRU order respects acquires,\n  and eviction returns one page per rank.\n- Isolated harness (full source plus upstream tests, only the import path\n  rewritten, `--noconftest`): **25 passed**, then a real localhost HTTP probe\n  that leases every complete key, asserts allocation fails, releases one key,\n  and asserts the eviction landed on that key.\n\n## Not verified here\n\n- Two vLLM instances sharing one metadata server on real 3FS (needs a 3FS setup;\n  @sivabreddy offered to run it).\n- The writer-side boundary with #61247.\n- Eviction cost and hit-rate impact: this PR is about correctness, not policy.\n- Connector-level unit tests were not run on the authoring machine (the review\n  environment has no torch/GPU); the connector change is small and was read by\n  hand. `pre-commit` has not been run yet either.\n\n## AI assistance\n\nThe implementation and this description were drafted with an AI coding agent\n(see the `Assisted-by` trailer). I am opening it as a draft for design feedback.\n",
